{"reference":"Acts 13","book":"Acts","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"The chapter begins with a group of prophets and teachers at Antioch worshiping, fasting, praying, and setting apart Barnabas and Saul before sending them out with John. This frames the journey as a shared undertaking within a community, rather than the work of a solitary founder. The chapter then repeatedly places Barnabas and Paul in synagogue settings among Jews, God-fearers, and other hearers; the turn to Gentiles at Pisidian Antioch does not erase that continuing Jewish setting or Paul’s Jewish identity.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:1-5","Acts 13:5-52"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_7f70f24ac27fb2708a476734"]}]},{"kind":"historical_context","title":"Synagogue Gathering","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The synagogue scene is more than a visit to a worship building. After readings from the law and the prophets, the synagogue leaders invite an address; Paul speaks, listeners ask to hear more the following Sabbath, and the gathering later becomes a setting for both response and dispute. In Luke and Acts, synagogues are places where Scripture is read and interpreted in a communal setting.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:14-43"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_ffade6a2ba24dee5b280c5d9"]}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"A Mixed Audience","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"Acts describes Israelites, God-worshipers, devout proselytes, Gentiles, women of standing, city leaders, and opponents in the episode at Pisidian Antioch. That range cautions against imagining either a uniform synagogue audience or a collective indictment of Jews. The narrative names particular conflict and particular responses, while also showing Jewish and Gentile participation around the message Paul proclaims.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:14-52"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_96073f860d63f797b30d9096"]}]},{"kind":"people_places","title":"From Antioch to Cyprus","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"The opening list presents Antioch as a leadership and mission center with a multilingual group of named teachers and prophets. On Cyprus, the encounter with Bar-Jesus, also called Elymas, is narrated as a contest over Sergius Paulus’s access to the message. The episode should not be turned into permission for violence against religious rivals.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:1-3","Acts 13:4-12"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_945786b561dd73705eae9b03"]}]},{"kind":"archaeology_geography","title":"The Journey to Pisidian Antioch","blocks":[{"id":"block_5","text":"Acts moves quickly from coastal Perga to highland Pisidian Antioch. Evidence for regional roads and Roman investment helps explain that these places were connected, and Pisidian Antioch was an important Roman colony in its wider Anatolian setting. But neither the chapter nor the surviving road evidence identifies every pass, stop, pace, or reason for the travelers’ exact route; detailed route maps go beyond what can be established.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:13-14","Acts 13:14-52"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_53ed108947aad034a3ba768a"]}]},{"kind":"language_literary","title":"The Prophetic Warning","blocks":[{"id":"block_6","text":"Paul’s warning in verse 41 receives Habakkuk 1:5 through a Greek textual tradition. Other New Testament writings receive a different Habakkuk passage, 2:3-4, for distinct rhetorical purposes. Here the quotation functions within Paul’s warning against rejecting the work he says God is doing.","verse_refs":["Acts 13:41"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_39183adf3f5d7d9e18722ef5"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Acts as a Text","blocks":[{"id":"block_7","text":"Acts survives in early papyri and major Greek manuscripts with differing textual profiles. Codex Bezae and related versional witnesses preserve a notably distinctive form of Acts, often called the Western text. Individual differences in that textual tradition need to be assessed case by case rather than treated as automatically original or automatically unimportant.","verse_refs":["Acts 10:1-48","Acts 11:1-30","Acts 12:1-25","Acts 14:1-28","Acts 15:1-41","Acts 16:1-40","Acts 17:1-34","Acts 18:1-28","Acts 19:1-41","Acts 1:1-26","Acts 20:1-38","Acts 21:1-40","Acts 22:1-30","Acts 23:1-35","Acts 24:1-27","Acts 25:1-27","Acts 26:1-32","Acts 27:1-44","Acts 28:1-31","Acts 2:1-47","Acts 3:1-26","Acts 4:1-37","Acts 5:1-42","Acts 6:1-15","Acts 7:1-60","Acts 8:1-40","Acts 9:1-43"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_85949b5a6332638952ccdf3d","reader_path_acts_013_3c48babf07d30204d1111501"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The Cities That Follow","blocks":[{"id":"block_8","text":"As the journey continues beyond this chapter, Pisidian Antioch, Iconium, Lystra, and Derbe should not be treated as one uniform civic or ethnic setting simply because Acts places them on the same journey. Likewise, Acts’ city sequence may be compared with Galatians, but it does not itself identify these assemblies as the letter’s recipients or provide a complete itinerary.","verse_refs":["Acts 14:1-21","Acts 14:1-28","Acts 15:1-41","Acts 16:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_acts_013_5406d82c8d5e35bb0aabb389","reader_path_acts_013_16e088569010bd886b09d0d7"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}